    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A popular conceptual model of fault evolution suggests that large faults develop by coalescence of smaller faults, implying that smaller faults may be coeval with or older than larger faults. Another model suggests that smaller faults may have formed due to stress perturbations resulting from slip on pre-existing larger faults, which implies that smaller faults must be younger . The normal fault array of the V olcanic Tableland, Bishop, CA, has been the site of several studies documenting growth by coalescence. However, subsidiary faulting appears plausible as well. Linear elastic models representing slip on various combinations of larger faults on and around the Tableland indicate that smaller faults are generally less abundant where the stress shadows of larger Tableland faults are greatest, suggesting that slip on larger faults does influence the initiation and growth of smaller faults. However, in the near surface where faults are thought to have initiated, stress perturbations are predominantly negative (tensile stress shadows). Thus, subsidiary faulting, in a strict sense, is not supported by models. The Tableland fault system likely initiated as a broad array of small faults, some of which have grown and coalesced to form larger faults, which in turn inhibit initiation of new faults and growth of existing small faults in reach of their stres shadows. However, the basin bounding White Mountain fault, and perhaps Fish Slough fault too, pre-dates the tuff, and its stress shadow appears to have inhibited extensional faulting west of Fish Slough fault since the fault system's inception.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
